1
00:00:00,611 --> 00:00:11,314
On July 16, 2022, Governor Baker signed into law an act relative to extending certain state of emergency accommodations, which among other things, extends the expiration of the provisions pertaining to the open meeting allowed to March 31, 2023.

2
00:00:11,755 --> 00:00:19,497
Specifically, this extension allows public bodies to continue holding meetings remotely without a quorum of the public body physically present at a meeting location.

3
00:00:20,097 --> 00:00:22,458
and to provide adequate alternative access to remote meetings.

4
00:00:22,998 --> 00:00:30,339
The act does not make any new changes to the open meeting law other than extending the expiration date of the temporary provisions regarding remote meetings from July 15, 2022 to March 31, 2023.

5
00:00:34,720 --> 00:00:35,300
Thanks, Dennis.

6
00:00:39,581 --> 00:00:48,903
OK, Dennis, did we have a description for what this meeting was that you could read out just to get us started before we hand it over to Judy?

7
00:00:50,153 --> 00:00:52,634
I think I've kept it pretty basic.

8
00:00:52,714 --> 00:01:05,400
Let me just... It's very simple.

9
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:07,641
Training on 40B for Board of Appeals members.

10
00:01:08,502 --> 00:01:08,882
Perfect.

11
00:01:08,982 --> 00:01:09,722
Thank you so much.

12
00:01:09,942 --> 00:01:11,403
Judy, all yours.

13
00:01:12,324 --> 00:01:12,584
Hi.

14
00:01:12,704 --> 00:01:13,484
So thank you.

15
00:01:13,784 --> 00:01:16,826
I'm Judy Barrett and I'm happy to be here.

16
00:01:17,642 --> 00:01:24,383
kind of involved with this a couple of years ago, and I know things have kind of been on hold and so forth for a while, so it's nice to be back.

17
00:01:25,724 --> 00:01:42,207
I do have a set of slides, which I suspect you probably expected anyway, just to sort of walk through a little bit of the history of the law and kind of how it works with the board, what happens before and after a project gets to the board,

18
00:01:43,096 --> 00:01:54,443
And what I thought I would do just to try to keep things moving, because I'm sure you're going to have questions, which is great, is that I tried to kind of create sort of three or four breaks in the slides to sort of stop and take questions.

19
00:01:55,303 --> 00:02:00,446
So if you have a question, if you could just hold it till I get to one of those breaks, that would be great.

20
00:02:01,007 --> 00:02:04,009
And I'll take as long as we need to to respond to questions.

21
00:02:04,969 --> 00:02:07,751
Just seems like it's probably a little easier to manage it this way.

22
00:02:07,831 --> 00:02:09,112
So just so you know who I am.

23
00:02:10,808 --> 00:02:20,362
I am a planner I've been in the field for about 35 years I have a small firm based in hang on there are nine folks who work with me at bear planning group.

24
00:02:21,129 --> 00:02:29,771
I've been working as a consultant to boards of appeals specifically on Chapter 40B matters for, oh gosh, probably close to 20 years.

25
00:02:30,811 --> 00:02:45,934
So I come at this mainly as a planner, although I think I know the law pretty well, but I'm gonna try to cover both some practical aspects of managing these cases and then also what the statutory and the regulatory requirements look like.

26
00:02:46,534 --> 00:02:50,635
So that's my plan and I think I'll go ahead and start screen sharing if that's okay.

27
00:02:51,484 --> 00:02:52,084
with you folks.

28
00:02:53,785 --> 00:02:54,305
That sounds great.

29
00:02:54,325 --> 00:02:54,645
Thank you.

30
00:02:55,485 --> 00:02:55,905
Thank you.

31
00:03:04,387 --> 00:03:05,107
Can you all see that?

32
00:03:06,207 --> 00:03:06,447
Yes.

33
00:03:07,288 --> 00:03:07,948
OK, super.

34
00:03:09,468 --> 00:03:17,970
So this is about working with the statute, and I call it that working with it for a reason, because it is it is certainly different from some other types of regulatory

35
00:03:18,822 --> 00:03:20,423
work that local officials do.

36
00:03:21,584 --> 00:03:25,546
It has some strengths, it has some weaknesses, and we'll kind of try to cover both.

37
00:03:27,127 --> 00:03:29,008
There are sort of four sections to this.

38
00:03:29,208 --> 00:03:35,171
One is just sort of an introduction to the statute, very basic material that probably all of you are familiar with.

39
00:03:36,492 --> 00:03:43,716
And then looking at how the process works from the application from a developer for what's called project eligibility.

40
00:03:44,956 --> 00:03:46,976
until the board reaches a decision.

41
00:03:46,996 --> 00:03:54,738
There are things that happen after the board has acted that sort of have to take place before a project even ends up under construction.

42
00:03:55,558 --> 00:04:03,779
And then just a little bit of information for all of you about some fair housing requirements from the Commonwealth that are fairly recent in the timeline of 40B.

43
00:04:04,839 --> 00:04:13,401
But sometimes people have questions about things that have appeared or been added into the state regulations over the last several years, and it's because of fair housing issues.

44
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:15,208
in large part.

45
00:04:17,553 --> 00:04:20,735
So this is sort of the kickoff to kind of working with the statute.

46
00:04:20,835 --> 00:04:29,140
And I always like to start with this slide because a lot of people don't realize that what Chapter 40B actually is, is the state's regional planning law.

47
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:43,529
So the same law that created Metropolitan Area Planning Council and all the other regional planning agencies we have in Massachusetts is also the parent legislation for what is probably just easily called the Affordable Housing Law or the Comprehensive Permit Law.

48
00:04:44,570 --> 00:04:56,151
But about five years after the Regional Planning Act was established, the legislature amended that law to add this provision that we all call Chapter 40 B, which is the affordable housing provision.

49
00:04:57,447 --> 00:05:06,756
And the focus of it and the reason it's in the regional planning law is that the intent of it is to address disparities in the regional distribution of affordable housing.

50
00:05:07,876 --> 00:05:09,198
I don't know how old you folks are.

51
00:05:09,698 --> 00:05:12,180
I'm old enough to remember some of this.

52
00:05:12,701 --> 00:05:22,829
And certainly around the time that the legislature adopted what we all call Chapter 40B, it was not just a coincidence.

53
00:05:22,850 --> 00:05:24,471
There were a lot of other things happening

54
00:05:25,019 --> 00:05:30,440
at the federal level with civil rights and fair housing that sort of this kind of relates to.

55
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:34,301
So there's a basic assumption in the statutory scheme.

56
00:05:35,781 --> 00:05:47,043
Unlike what a lot of people think, the law doesn't say thou shalt have 10% of your housing stock affordable, or thou shalt have 1.5% of your land area occupied by lower moderate income housing.

57
00:05:47,863 --> 00:05:49,683
What it says is that these are four thresholds.

58
00:05:50,303 --> 00:05:54,084
So if you meet one of them, the presumption in favor of the city is

59
00:05:54,938 --> 00:05:58,421
you have met your regional fair share, whatever that may be.

60
00:05:59,021 --> 00:06:13,572
So I call these provisions in the statute the statutory safe harbors because they have a particular meaning in law that I think in some ways is even stronger than the regulatory safe harbors that we'll get to a little bit later in this discussion.

61
00:06:15,773 --> 00:06:17,335
I'm sure you're all familiar with this.

62
00:06:17,535 --> 00:06:20,537
There are three statutory minima so-called in the law.

63
00:06:21,298 --> 00:06:23,779
The most commonly referred to one is the 10% minimum.

64
00:06:25,318 --> 00:06:30,483
And there are going to be times in this presentation where I may default to that just because it's the most common one.

65
00:06:31,003 --> 00:06:35,227
You should feel free to stop me at the question and answer time and say, well, how does that relate to us?

66
00:06:36,508 --> 00:06:38,670
You know, when we're asserting that 1.5%.

67
00:06:39,430 --> 00:06:42,353
But I think the basic assumptions are the same.

68
00:06:42,473 --> 00:06:49,899
If you meet one of these statutory minima, then you're deemed to have met your regional fair share.

69
00:06:51,436 --> 00:06:54,678
don't meet one of them, then you're presumed to have unmet need.

70
00:06:55,359 --> 00:06:58,581
And that's not unmet local need, it's unmet regional need.

71
00:06:59,482 --> 00:07:03,684
And I'm going to continue to sort of emphasize that because that's why it's in the regional planning law.

72
00:07:03,885 --> 00:07:06,646
It's really about regional distribution of affordable housing.

73
00:07:08,408 --> 00:07:11,310
So I just put these up just as a frame of reference.

74
00:07:11,470 --> 00:07:13,011
It's not to compare communities.

75
00:07:13,491 --> 00:07:14,412
I do a lot of work in

76
00:07:15,325 --> 00:07:16,746
the immediate Boston area.

77
00:07:16,946 --> 00:07:19,849
And some of these towns have just gotten to 10%.

78
00:07:19,909 --> 00:07:25,413
Brookline has struggled for, oh my gosh, probably six or seven years to get just over 10%.

79
00:07:26,554 --> 00:07:31,298
Heaven knows where they'll be when the census 2020, the rest of the census 2020 data comes out.

80
00:07:31,358 --> 00:07:35,881
But some of these are at over 10%, some are under.

81
00:07:36,282 --> 00:07:40,585
Some of them I'm sure you'll recognize as communities that even if they're, quote, under the 10%,

82
00:07:41,966 --> 00:07:45,248
they've asserted that they meet the general land area minimum.

83
00:07:45,328 --> 00:07:47,129
So I'm not saying they do or don't.

84
00:07:47,189 --> 00:07:58,455
I'm just saying that there are a few communities on this list that you have to be a little careful when you say, well, Somerville is only at nine point seven percent because Somerville asserts that it reads the addresses the general land area minimum.

85
00:07:59,055 --> 00:08:04,678
The point, I think, really, in putting this up is just to sort of assure you that everybody's kind of struggling with this.

86
00:08:05,438 --> 00:08:13,725
and some communities have just barely gotten to the 10%, some communities are significantly over, and some are still working toward it.

87
00:08:14,346 --> 00:08:29,019
So it's not uncommon for communities to perhaps find themselves not having received any comprehensive permanent applications for a long, long time, and then all of a sudden be hit with several, which is partially what happened, I think, with Medford.

88
00:08:29,079 --> 00:08:32,682
So when a community kind of

89
00:08:33,743 --> 00:08:37,446
falls below that minimum, there are certain things that sort of become true.

90
00:08:37,686 --> 00:08:54,879
Whatever minimum we're talking about, GLAM or 10%, the developers who are applying for a comprehensive permit are kind of assumed to have an eligibility to request waivers of local regulations.

91
00:08:54,919 --> 00:09:00,123
So there's an assumption that if they could build the project economically, they wouldn't be in front of you for a comprehensive permit.

92
00:09:01,869 --> 00:09:14,214
The task then for the Zoning Board of Appeals is to balance the regional need for housing, which is the emphasis of the statute, against a series of local concerns, which are also laid out in the statute.

93
00:09:14,794 --> 00:09:23,137
And the law presumes that that regional need is paramount if you are under the statutory minimum or any, you know, any one of them.

94
00:09:24,334 --> 00:09:29,656
The burden then falls to the city to show that local concerns outweigh those regional needs.

95
00:09:30,616 --> 00:09:37,138
And sometimes that permitting process, trying to work this out with the developer, can feel a little bit like a tug of war.

96
00:09:38,219 --> 00:09:39,219
And sometimes it doesn't.

97
00:09:39,279 --> 00:09:40,700
Sometimes it's just very smooth.

98
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,201
So the safe harbor is in the law.

99
00:09:44,921 --> 00:09:50,763
If you meet one of the statutory minima, you're still going to get applications for comprehensive permits.

100
00:09:52,203 --> 00:09:53,524
We wouldn't be the only community

101
00:09:54,510 --> 00:09:55,011
to do that.

102
00:09:55,371 --> 00:09:58,534
There's a pretty famous case from the town of Amherst on this very issue.

103
00:09:59,234 --> 00:10:05,319
I happen to be working right now with the town of Foxborough, which is well over 10%, and they're still entertaining a comprehensive permit.

104
00:10:05,379 --> 00:10:12,125
So the fact that a city or town meets one of the statutory minima doesn't mean that quote 40B goes away.

105
00:10:12,605 --> 00:10:14,046
Developers may still apply.

106
00:10:15,027 --> 00:10:22,614
And your jurisdiction really doesn't change in terms of what the scope of the local concerns is under the statute.

107
00:10:23,561 --> 00:10:35,347
But what does kind of shift is that the board is in a stronger position to insist on or I'll say insist on the applicant to

108
00:10:36,073 --> 00:10:41,175
to meet more of the local regulations to address some or all of those local concerns.

109
00:10:41,495 --> 00:10:47,797
It becomes easier for the board to make those, to insist on that when you meet a statutory minimum.

110
00:10:48,437 --> 00:10:56,741
And of course, I think the most obvious piece is that the developer really cannot at that point go to the housing appeals committee.

111
00:10:56,781 --> 00:10:59,702
It's really their developer status is the same as anybody else's.

112
00:11:00,542 --> 00:11:03,423
If they're unhappy with their decision, they have to go to court.

113
00:11:05,748 --> 00:11:07,229
So that's the safe harbor law.

114
00:11:07,309 --> 00:11:12,253
The safe harbor regulations are a creature, if you will, of regulation.

115
00:11:13,514 --> 00:11:22,941
What we have today that create a framework for towns or cities to get some credit for working toward that, in this case, it's really the 10%.

116
00:11:25,443 --> 00:11:28,225
This all comes out of regulatory changes that happened in 2001.

117
00:11:30,491 --> 00:11:36,476
when the market in Massachusetts had pretty much recovered from the recession of 10 years earlier.

118
00:11:36,496 --> 00:11:39,218
And there was a lot of housing development happening.

119
00:11:39,518 --> 00:11:48,945
And as a result, a lot of communities approaching census 2000 fell below 10%, even if they had been at it for most of a decade.

120
00:11:49,906 --> 00:11:59,073
And so the state in an effort to, you know, sort of soften the blow on communities adopted a set of regulations that said, if you do certain things,

121
00:12:00,005 --> 00:12:08,347
you can have a reprieve from having to consider a lot of comprehensive permits all at once or at least one case here or something very large.

122
00:12:08,407 --> 00:12:13,268
And the one that's the most well-known is the so-called certified housing production plan.

123
00:12:14,208 --> 00:12:24,371
My understanding is that the city has a housing production plan that's been prepared and has not yet been submitted to the Department of Housing and Community Development for approval.

124
00:12:25,211 --> 00:12:27,872
Therefore, it really doesn't help the ZBA at all.

125
00:12:27,952 --> 00:12:29,492
But if the plan is submitted,

126
00:12:30,218 --> 00:12:54,365
And if it is approved, which I'm sure it probably will be, because I know the consultant well who prepared it, if it's approved, then anything that you do after that for the five-year lifeline that the certification or the approval is valid, any units created once that plan's approved put you in a position as a community to say, okay, we've hit a certain goal.

127
00:12:54,785 --> 00:12:56,286
We can just slow down a little bit.

128
00:12:57,518 --> 00:13:01,481
I'm embarrassed to tell you, right off the top of my head, I don't remember what Medford's goal is, but you probably know.

129
00:13:03,002 --> 00:13:03,963
It's a production goal.

130
00:13:04,003 --> 00:13:10,447
So it's saying, really, we want you to work toward, and in this case, it really is the 10% minimum, we want you to work toward this.

131
00:13:11,088 --> 00:13:19,174
And if you do, and you make enough progress, then you can take a break from approving comprehensive permits, or you can keep approving, it's totally up to you.

132
00:13:19,354 --> 00:13:24,898
And the maximum period for which a community is eligible for certification is two years.

133
00:13:26,332 --> 00:13:35,817
For communities that don't have a housing production plan, there's still an opportunity to get some credit, if you will, for producing a lot of new affordable housing.

134
00:13:35,857 --> 00:13:37,357
It's called the recent progress rule.

135
00:13:37,998 --> 00:13:40,819
So the number of units that you have to hit is higher.

136
00:13:41,739 --> 00:13:46,582
However, you don't necessarily have to have a housing plan to trigger that.

137
00:13:47,462 --> 00:13:50,664
There's also a standard, which probably isn't going to help Medford.

138
00:13:50,724 --> 00:13:52,425
It's really meant for smaller towns.

139
00:13:53,065 --> 00:13:55,486
It's called a large-scale project cap.

140
00:13:56,143 --> 00:14:06,210
The Commonwealth has established some standards for how big a given development can be in order for it to be, in order for the Board of Appeals to essentially have to consider it.

141
00:14:07,491 --> 00:14:12,354
And that kind of goes up to as high as 350 units, or in some cases, even higher.

142
00:14:13,695 --> 00:14:24,382
But there's an assumption here that when a project is applied to a Board of Appeals, and it's just simply too big for the board, for the town or city to absorb, that the board could turn it down.

143
00:14:25,492 --> 00:14:36,519
And then there's finally this concept of the related application, which is essentially a developer has applied for some approval from the city under your zoning or some other provision and is turned down.

144
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:41,583
But the developer can't just come back now and say, I'm going to file a spike 40B.

145
00:14:42,063 --> 00:14:51,129
There has to be this sort of cooling off periods, we call it, of at least 12 months before the applicant can come to the Board of Appeals and seek a comprehensive permit.

146
00:14:51,867 --> 00:14:54,328
So those are kind of the regulatory safe harbors.

147
00:14:54,908 --> 00:14:58,969
Again, the assumption is if you meet any of these, then you're entitled to the benefits.

148
00:14:59,510 --> 00:15:03,111
But those benefits are established in regulation, not in the statute.

149
00:15:03,991 --> 00:15:12,114
I may draw more of a distinction between what the statute says and what the regulations say than many other people do, but I think it's an important distinction.

150
00:15:12,194 --> 00:15:14,675
So just want to make that clear.

151
00:15:17,616 --> 00:15:20,938
People ask me all the time, well, what is affordable housing under Chapter 40B?

152
00:15:20,998 --> 00:15:24,040
Because this also is an issue in a lot of the towns I've worked in.

153
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:37,746
The easiest way to answer the question is, it's a housing that the Department of Housing and Community Development has decided will count towards the 10% goal for every community.

154
00:15:38,747 --> 00:15:45,090
And if that housing counts, it's because it is affordable to households whose incomes are at or below

155
00:15:46,272 --> 00:15:47,853
80% of the area median income.

156
00:15:48,453 --> 00:15:50,975
And those income limits are determined by HUD.

157
00:15:51,115 --> 00:15:52,416
They are updated every year.

158
00:15:53,557 --> 00:15:57,900
Just for the sake of your own information, this is kind of what the income limits look like right now.

159
00:15:58,761 --> 00:16:09,769
So a household of two with income below $53,700 a year would be considered very low income and so forth.

160
00:16:09,849 --> 00:16:14,092
So the income limits, as you can see, are adjusted for household size.

161
00:16:16,017 --> 00:16:24,299
The standard under 40 be is at or below 80% of area median income and I don't know your community as well as I know some others I've worked in.

162
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:31,081
But sometimes that is a pretty high number in terms of the actual need that exists in the community.

163
00:16:33,857 --> 00:16:38,278
of appeals doesn't really have the jurisdiction to decide what the income limits will be.

164
00:16:38,338 --> 00:16:47,000
It's kind of up to the subsidizing agency, but that's not to say you can't ask the developer to try to work with you on something that's more affordable.

165
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:54,962
Before I get into project eligibility, does anybody have any questions that you want to ask right now, or do you want me to just keep going for a bit?

166
00:16:57,974 --> 00:17:00,016
I'm fine for you to keep going unless there are any questions.

167
00:17:00,056 --> 00:17:01,697
I don't see any raised hands at this time.

168
00:17:02,458 --> 00:17:02,778
Okay.

169
00:17:02,798 --> 00:17:07,522
Judy, do you want me to just give you that update on the housing production plan that was the meeting?

170
00:17:07,582 --> 00:17:08,423
Yeah, that'd be great.

171
00:17:08,443 --> 00:17:08,663
Sure.

172
00:17:09,184 --> 00:17:12,407
So we had a committee of the whole with the city council this evening.

173
00:17:13,354 --> 00:17:17,998
And we offered a few minor amendments to the existing plan.

174
00:17:18,398 --> 00:17:19,780
They were very happy with it.

175
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:33,091
We just changed some of the wording to more accurately reflect what we meant, and it made them a little more comfortable with a few of the actions that we would study, not just do certain things.

176
00:17:33,551 --> 00:17:34,352
You never do anything.

177
00:17:34,372 --> 00:17:35,373
You always study it first.

178
00:17:36,713 --> 00:17:48,058
And they voted it out of committee, and they invited us to submit the revised clean copy for their city council meeting that's in two weeks, where they have every expectation they'll approve it.

179
00:17:49,139 --> 00:17:49,559
Excellent.

180
00:17:49,599 --> 00:17:50,059
That's great.

181
00:17:50,079 --> 00:17:59,523
Because you certainly want to get that plan in and get it approved as soon as you can for the board to be able to enjoy the benefits of that.

182
00:18:02,491 --> 00:18:08,854
So why don't I just go ahead then and talk, go into this whole process that goes on before the applications reach you.

183
00:18:09,455 --> 00:18:13,957
This is the project eligibility process, sometimes called site approval.

184
00:18:15,278 --> 00:18:28,104
The developer has to apply to a housing subsidizing agency for what we fondly call a PEL or project eligibility letter before coming to the board of appeals for a comprehensive permit.

185
00:18:29,894 --> 00:18:36,217
The subsidizing agencies are required to let you know that they've received an application for project eligibility.

186
00:18:36,977 --> 00:18:38,878
They are required to do a site visit.

187
00:18:39,178 --> 00:18:52,583
Usually that is done by contacting the city staff to arrange a site visit so that if the state officials come out to see the property, they have someone local they can speak with and ask questions of.

188
00:18:53,704 --> 00:18:56,365
They do go through a review process with the applications.

189
00:18:57,405 --> 00:18:58,906
Sometimes this takes quite a while,

190
00:19:00,064 --> 00:19:07,266
I've seen project eligibility review processes go on for nine months, and then sometimes they move pretty quickly.

191
00:19:07,987 --> 00:19:23,731
But during that process of review by the subsidizing agency, they're supposed to get an appraisal to determine the fair market value of the property, not taking into account any additional density to be achieved through the comprehensive permit.

192
00:19:25,232 --> 00:19:29,133
And then there are a series of other standards of review, which I'll get to it momentarily.

193
00:19:30,526 --> 00:19:34,948
I think it's important to understand that the subsidizing agency is not a permitting authority.

194
00:19:35,088 --> 00:19:51,356
One of the things that drives a lot of communities crazy is that they submit comments to the subsidizing agency and then feel like they weren't heard because the developer still comes forth with the application pretty much the way the community had objected to it in the first place.

195
00:19:52,576 --> 00:19:55,578
And I think the position of state government is,

196
00:19:56,406 --> 00:20:05,551
If we were the ones deciding whether these projects should go forward, we would probably have World War Three in our hands from local officials saying the Commonwealth shouldn't be making those decisions.

197
00:20:05,631 --> 00:20:08,632
So they sort of take the view that we're not a permitting authority.

198
00:20:08,692 --> 00:20:13,234
We're really just sort of making a determination of eligibility for something to go forward.

199
00:20:14,595 --> 00:20:16,336
And so they issue a decision.

200
00:20:16,869 --> 00:20:27,198
in writing if they decide that the project should go forward and come to the Board of Appeals, and that it's really your job to regulate it in whatever way you see fit.

201
00:20:27,418 --> 00:20:44,893
So that PEL, Project Eligibility Letter, really sort of opens the door for the developer to come to the Board of Appeals, or in the occasional time when a PEL is turned down or simply not acted on, it closes the door for an application to come forward.

202
00:20:46,296 --> 00:20:53,520
So these sort of seven questions are really what the subsidizing agency has to address in some way.

203
00:20:53,700 --> 00:20:56,681
And I'm not going to tell you it's an in-depth review.

204
00:20:56,821 --> 00:20:58,662
I will tell you that this is the checklist.

205
00:20:59,923 --> 00:21:08,026
They have to decide, is the project that they're looking at sort of eligible under whatever subsidy program guidelines the developer is claiming to be working under?

206
00:21:08,187 --> 00:21:11,488
So the most common one, I think, is the New England Fund.

207
00:21:12,709 --> 00:21:15,090
And so that often becomes the sort of standard.

208
00:21:16,009 --> 00:21:18,150
they're not particularly tight requirements.

209
00:21:19,611 --> 00:21:22,312
Is the site generally appropriate for residential development?

210
00:21:23,232 --> 00:21:27,894
Sometimes communities bristle about this because the site might be in an industrial district.

211
00:21:28,634 --> 00:21:39,819
But I think it's important to remember that the statute considers, for purposes of general land area minimum calculation, any land that is zoned for residential, commercial, or industrial development.

212
00:21:39,879 --> 00:21:44,201
So that kind of becomes the standard for, is this at its most basic level

213
00:21:44,700 --> 00:21:46,460
appropriate for residential development.

214
00:21:47,601 --> 00:21:50,941
Is the project design generally appropriate?

215
00:21:51,161 --> 00:21:58,522
The Commonwealth has design guidelines which are intended for use by the subsidizing agencies, not by local boards.

216
00:21:59,323 --> 00:22:00,263
They're fairly broad.

217
00:22:01,343 --> 00:22:10,365
I don't know of too many cases where projects were kind of resculpted during the PEL process because of design considerations.

218
00:22:10,525 --> 00:22:12,925
I do know that it has been done once in a while.

219
00:22:14,205 --> 00:22:15,566
Does the project seem feasible?

220
00:22:16,587 --> 00:22:21,591
That is a very kind of general look at would this project make sense in the market where it's located?

221
00:22:21,691 --> 00:22:27,516
So there is a kind of a basic look at some market data as part of the PEL process.

222
00:22:28,937 --> 00:22:31,479
Does it look like it's financially feasible, et cetera?

223
00:22:32,020 --> 00:22:33,281
Is the applicant eligible?

224
00:22:33,381 --> 00:22:40,887
And all that means is, is this applicant a public agency, a nonprofit organization, or a private for-profit developer?

225
00:22:41,509 --> 00:22:47,371
who will enter into a limited dividend agreement to essentially cap the profits taken out of the development?

226
00:22:48,191 --> 00:22:50,011
And then does the applicant have site control?

227
00:22:50,231 --> 00:22:55,393
And so those last couple of pieces, the eligibility and the site control, those are actually like right out of the statute.

228
00:22:56,233 --> 00:23:01,795
So what the subsidizing agency is doing is saying, we're going to take responsibility for making those determinations.

229
00:23:02,175 --> 00:23:03,595
You don't have to do it, ZBA.

230
00:23:04,015 --> 00:23:10,357
That said, most ZBAs I know take a look at that anyway, because the statute really directs them to do so.

231
00:23:12,538 --> 00:23:18,862
The local responsibilities during project eligibility are to assist with making that site visit happen.

232
00:23:20,223 --> 00:23:30,250
To comment, the project eligibility process includes a minimum 30-day comment period for the municipality.

233
00:23:30,330 --> 00:23:38,796
So town departments typically provide comments to the mayor or the select board, whatever the form of government is in the particular community.

234
00:23:40,015 --> 00:23:44,257
Boards that might normally review one of these projects may also submit comments.

235
00:23:45,418 --> 00:23:57,945
My experience is it generally makes the most sense for all of those local comments to be incorporated into one letter to the subsidizing agency instead of, you know, barraging them with several letters.

236
00:23:58,625 --> 00:24:04,488
But it's just a more organized approach I think is more effective, but that's just my experience.

237
00:24:05,028 --> 00:24:08,330
In any case, the point is that when that application comes in,

238
00:24:08,978 --> 00:24:21,627
and you are notified when the city is notified about it, it's important to get it out to the people who would normally be reviewing it to permit it so they can provide comments to the chief elected official that will then go back to the subsidizing agency.

239
00:24:23,189 --> 00:24:34,497
And then, I'm sorry, I said select board, I should also note mayor, those comments get submitted to the subsidizing agency and they are considered, it's not uncommon

240
00:24:35,605 --> 00:24:47,890
for a project eligibility letter to include an attachment that kind of lists all of the concerns in a summary kind of way that came from the municipality and a request from the subsidizing agency to the developer to address them.

241
00:24:48,671 --> 00:25:00,455
I won't tell you that they're always addressed, but the subsidizing agency, I think at least is sort of making an attempt to document what was said so that the developer is on notice.

242
00:25:00,475 --> 00:25:03,857
These are things that are probably going to come at you during the review process.

243
00:25:06,929 --> 00:25:16,561
So, this is a place where again I could stop for a minute if you want, or I can keep going because I'm going to talk about the how this permit process works at your level.

244
00:25:18,183 --> 00:25:19,845
Yeah, Judy, I have a question.

245
00:25:19,865 --> 00:25:25,633
Just one question so in terms of project eligibility.

246
00:25:26,596 --> 00:25:36,001
One area that I'm confused about is kind of how final that determination is at the time it comes before the ZBA.

247
00:25:36,662 --> 00:25:51,810
Just to give some examples, like I've heard of concepts surrounding zoning boards asking for pro forma review if they would like more affordable housing units or

248
00:25:53,835 --> 00:26:04,328
to put a local preference condition and things of that nature, all of which seem like things that the developer would then have to go back to the subsidizing agency for.

249
00:26:04,428 --> 00:26:07,672
But as described, it feels very final.

250
00:26:07,713 --> 00:26:11,357
Like they've already applied, they went through the process, and now it's done.

251
00:26:11,457 --> 00:26:12,559
So which one is it?

252
00:26:13,600 --> 00:26:32,267
So, their position typically this has come up in many places I've worked, something changes with the application or the board asked something to bring something out that nobody knew the subsidizing agencies seem to take the position, and I am being a bit general here but seem to take the position that unless there's some fundamental

253
00:26:33,907 --> 00:26:40,718
reason to reopen the PEO review, they will look at whatever the developer negotiated with the municipality.

254
00:26:41,299 --> 00:26:48,490
If the project comes back to them for what's called final approval, which is a whole process that takes place after the comprehensive permit is granted.

255
00:26:49,133 --> 00:26:50,914
And I'm going to talk about that a little bit later.

256
00:26:50,934 --> 00:26:54,897
But generally, the subsidizing agencies don't want to get back involved with this.

257
00:26:54,917 --> 00:27:06,486
They sort of determined that it's really the Zoning Board of Appeals job at that point to review a project based on local concerns and get what they can.

258
00:27:07,307 --> 00:27:13,292
There are some jurisdictional issues with what you just said that I hate to be the bearer of bad news.

259
00:27:15,448 --> 00:27:25,450
The current state policy does allow a municipality to request up to 70% of the affordable units to be allocated on what's called a local preference basis.

260
00:27:26,150 --> 00:27:27,210
It's not guaranteed.

261
00:27:27,590 --> 00:27:29,131
It is a request that gets made.

262
00:27:30,311 --> 00:27:36,052
It's not really the ZBA's job as much as it is sort of the policy officials in the city to make that determination.

263
00:27:37,452 --> 00:27:39,293
But where it gets a little more difficult is

264
00:27:41,106 --> 00:27:48,312
If the developer is applying for a traditional 40 B and the units are going to be affordable at 80% of median income.

265
00:27:49,033 --> 00:27:51,695
And the board says wait a minute you know our local needs are different.

266
00:27:52,195 --> 00:27:59,721
We need lower income housing, which I hear often, especially in the Boston area because the income limits are so crazy high.

267
00:28:01,145 --> 00:28:03,546
That's really the subsidizing agency's jurisdiction.

268
00:28:04,467 --> 00:28:16,732
So it becomes kind of difficult for the board to be the one receiving this testimony from the community that we really need more deeply affordable housing, but it's the subsidizing agency's jurisdiction.

269
00:28:16,812 --> 00:28:28,597
So if you try to impose on the project a set of income limits, I can almost assure you that that decision would be overturned.

270
00:28:30,030 --> 00:28:39,817
but I certainly have seen plenty of boards negotiate with developers to make, for example, a project that was submitted as 80% to make the affordable units 50%.

271
00:28:40,177 --> 00:28:47,162
The percentage of affordable units might come down, but the income limit would be closer to what the city or the town is looking for.

272
00:28:47,182 --> 00:28:48,904
I have seen that done.

273
00:28:49,104 --> 00:28:51,986
I just don't wanna tell you that you have the jurisdiction to force it.

274
00:28:52,926 --> 00:28:56,669
Sure, yeah, and to be clear, I didn't frame the question in that

275
00:28:57,503 --> 00:29:23,211
manner and like forcing the issue but so it sounds like if over the course of the hearing there was some discussion and negotiation and the developer was amenable to certain changes in those areas that the mechanism by which that would then get sort of decided is there's this final review in front of the subsidizing agency.

276
00:29:24,153 --> 00:29:40,522
So if your permit said, we want 20% and 50% of AMI, and the developer agreed to it, so there's no appeal, the probability is the subsidizing agency, assuming everything else is consistent with their program requirements, would allow that to go forward.

277
00:29:40,862 --> 00:29:42,643
I've actually seen that a couple of times.

278
00:29:43,263 --> 00:29:48,466
Where it gets tricky is when you're trying to mix income tiers, some at 80%, some at 60%, some at 50%.

279
00:29:48,506 --> 00:29:49,547
That generally is much harder.

280
00:29:52,395 --> 00:30:04,065
But if you're saying we just think our need is different, if the developer is trying to work with you and they think they can make it work financially, they'll try.

281
00:30:04,085 --> 00:30:08,248
I just want to make it clear that the board doesn't have the jurisdiction to require it.

282
00:30:08,989 --> 00:30:15,234
I also want to point out, though, that sometimes things come up in the hearing process that really ought to be bumped back to the subsidizing agency.

283
00:30:15,314 --> 00:30:16,776
And a classic example is

284
00:30:19,319 --> 00:30:21,020
the developer has to maintain site approval.

285
00:30:21,520 --> 00:30:24,160
I mean, excuse me, site eligibility, site control.

286
00:30:25,101 --> 00:30:39,125
Well, if the board of appeals finds out that the purchase and sale agreement expired six months before, that becomes something that the board should be concerned about because the project is supposed to be in front of you with an applicant that's got site control.

287
00:30:39,865 --> 00:30:41,966
Now, I've seen that come up as well.

288
00:30:42,126 --> 00:30:45,987
And typically, the subsidizing agencies will say, we'll deal with it later.

289
00:30:46,682 --> 00:30:56,952
But in one case I was directly involved with, it did create an issue back to the subsidizing agency because there clearly had been a lapse in site control.

290
00:30:57,032 --> 00:31:01,696
And there was a real question whether the developer was going to be able to continue.

291
00:31:02,076 --> 00:31:11,345
So I guess the easiest answer is the subsidizing agency generally try to step back and let you work out what you can with the developer.

292
00:31:12,918 --> 00:31:16,642
And if it all works out between all of you guys, then it gets back to them.

293
00:31:16,662 --> 00:31:24,391
They would, I think, be inclined to just accept it unless it created some programmatic inconsistency for them.

294
00:31:24,411 --> 00:31:27,014
Does that make sense?

295
00:31:27,715 --> 00:31:28,075
It does.

296
00:31:28,175 --> 00:31:28,516
Thank you.

297
00:31:29,767 --> 00:31:31,948
Lisa, do you have your hand up?

298
00:31:31,968 --> 00:31:32,649
I do.

299
00:31:32,769 --> 00:31:42,555
Because of what you were just discussing, the deeper affordability, because that is something that we have discussed with some of these developers.

300
00:31:42,575 --> 00:31:47,458
And so you might have clarified it when you said, well, if we want

301
00:31:48,199 --> 00:32:01,118
20% 20% at 50% am I, instead of 25% at 80%, that's that could just go through and not be a big deal, but if we wanted a combination that gets tricky, really hard.

302
00:32:01,438 --> 00:32:01,619
We.

303
00:32:03,310 --> 00:32:08,973
asked about it and the developers that we discussed it with said, we're not allowed to do that.

304
00:32:09,493 --> 00:32:10,754
We won't get that approved.

305
00:32:11,374 --> 00:32:25,802
And that was a question whether, and that may be something that we should take offline and discuss, whether it's that it won't get approved or it's that the math is hard and that gets very complicated.

306
00:32:26,742 --> 00:32:28,343
Well, the math could be a problem.

307
00:32:28,463 --> 00:32:31,885
And if it makes the project an economic, that's a different issue.

308
00:32:33,732 --> 00:32:45,155
I have seen developers accept, obviously with the blessing of the subsidizing agency, but I have certainly seen that percentage of affordable units change and the income limits change.

309
00:32:45,375 --> 00:32:48,335
It doesn't happen often, but I have seen it happen.

310
00:32:49,515 --> 00:32:49,795
Okay.

311
00:32:50,296 --> 00:33:01,878
Maybe something worthy of us discussing just because it's something that is of a lot of need in Medford is the deeper affordability, but to cut the number of units

312
00:33:03,622 --> 00:33:08,183
that are affordable in order to get deeper affordability might not be the right choice.

313
00:33:08,803 --> 00:33:10,684
That's why we were asking for a mix.

314
00:33:12,704 --> 00:33:17,326
The mix is going to be much harder for them to sell, you know, to do.

315
00:33:17,986 --> 00:33:23,087
But the regulations clearly provide for 20% and 50% or 25% and 80%.

316
00:33:23,427 --> 00:33:30,809
Where this can get a little bit difficult is if the project eligibility letters come from an agency that doesn't have a 50% program,

317
00:33:33,119 --> 00:33:36,060
then the developer is not going to be able to do what you're asking.

318
00:33:36,700 --> 00:33:41,061
But that could be another reason why they might say, I don't know where their PELs are coming from.

319
00:33:41,081 --> 00:33:42,781
I don't know any of that.

320
00:33:42,921 --> 00:33:50,203
So just generally, I would say, if it's possible, and they think they can make it work financially, I've seen it done.

321
00:33:51,783 --> 00:33:52,343
I've seen it done.

322
00:33:52,363 --> 00:33:53,303
That's the best I can say.

323
00:33:54,324 --> 00:33:54,664
Thank you.

324
00:33:54,724 --> 00:33:55,304
It's good to know.

325
00:33:56,264 --> 00:33:56,424
Yeah.

326
00:33:56,884 --> 00:33:58,785
This has come up in a lot of towns, Alicia.

327
00:34:01,535 --> 00:34:02,956
communities right around Boston.

328
00:34:03,036 --> 00:34:17,345
But even on the periphery of the Boston metro area, when you get out to those towns, for 80% of AMI for the Middlesex County, the Boston metro area, makes no sense at all in those communities.

329
00:34:17,905 --> 00:34:21,667
And so there is a lot of frustration around, how do we meet the needs of our own residents?

330
00:34:24,049 --> 00:34:27,271
I mean, another possibility, which is really beyond the Board of Appeals,

331
00:34:28,551 --> 00:34:39,662
jurisdiction or problem, but there have been a few cases I know of where communities have invested money in bringing down 80% units to 60%, say, for example.

332
00:34:41,284 --> 00:34:48,911
Essentially becoming a lending source to the project, but getting some more affordable units because they now have a financial role in the project.

333
00:34:50,633 --> 00:34:51,453
Seen that done too.

334
00:34:52,154 --> 00:34:53,055
Not often, but done.

335
00:34:54,906 --> 00:34:59,269
So why don't I go on into this, and if you guys are okay with that, that sort of permitting process.

336
00:34:59,289 --> 00:35:00,130
Is that all right?

337
00:35:00,991 --> 00:35:01,251
Sure.

338
00:35:01,631 --> 00:35:02,072
Thank you.

339
00:35:02,092 --> 00:35:02,312
Okay.

340
00:35:02,832 --> 00:35:03,052
Sure.

341
00:35:05,134 --> 00:35:10,558
So I know you are already familiar with this, but I just have to say it because I get asked this a lot.

342
00:35:11,339 --> 00:35:14,141
There are big differences between rental and ownership projects.

343
00:35:15,602 --> 00:35:23,568
For rental developments where 25% of the units are low income or 20%, very low income, all the units count on the subsidized housing inventory.

344
00:35:24,302 --> 00:35:25,802
including the market rate units.

345
00:35:26,302 --> 00:35:28,583
That's not the case with homeownership developments.

346
00:35:29,843 --> 00:35:36,605
Some communities have wanted desperately for the developer to change their projects to rental in order to get all the units to count.

347
00:35:37,285 --> 00:35:40,386
And again, this is a jurisdictional issue I just want you to be aware of.

348
00:35:40,866 --> 00:35:42,766
It's the developer's calls, not the board's.

349
00:35:44,447 --> 00:35:53,389
So just know that there is a benefit, I guess, if you will, or an incentive to doing rental developments in terms of the contribution to

350
00:35:54,297 --> 00:36:02,042
the subsidized housing inventory, or I really suppose even the way the general land area calculation is done.

351
00:36:04,063 --> 00:36:11,828
The scope of the comprehensive permit is that you sort of become the board for a lot of things that you normally don't do.

352
00:36:13,182 --> 00:36:28,269
And this is one of the reasons why, when I've worked with CBAs, I've tried to sort of get the conservation commission and the planning board and anybody else who's got some role to play in the site, if the site were not a comp permit, to try to get them involved in helping with the review.

353
00:36:28,369 --> 00:36:34,392
Because often CBAs find themselves looking at something that's completely alien to their normal work.

354
00:36:36,251 --> 00:37:00,795
But any local regulations administered by local boards, zoning is an obvious one, subdivision control, I don't even know if Medford sees subdivisions anymore, but if there's a local wetlands bylaw, a historic district or scenic roads, those elements that the community has the ability to adopt local regulations for, that becomes the board's job.

355
00:37:01,455 --> 00:37:03,556
You are sitting in the role of all those boards.

356
00:37:04,216 --> 00:37:05,596
And you gain the authority

357
00:37:06,589 --> 00:37:11,513
to grant waivers from those local regulations in order to allow the project to go forward.

358
00:37:12,794 --> 00:37:23,783
Naturally, the Conservation Commission for a project that has any wetland interest involved, the Conservation Commission is still going to act under the State Wetlands Act, Chapter 131, Section 40.

359
00:37:24,564 --> 00:37:25,705
That does not go away.

360
00:37:25,825 --> 00:37:30,389
It's strictly when you have a local wetlands ordinance, but that sort of falls to the CBA.

361
00:37:31,958 --> 00:37:39,300
So there are things that are not within your scope and I think I just, it's actually kind of helpful I think to the board to say we can't touch that.

362
00:37:40,520 --> 00:37:52,643
People often bring up things like what's this going to do to our schools, you know, what's the fiscal impact of this going to be, how do we know who's going to live in the developments, how much money is the developer going to make.

363
00:37:53,252 --> 00:37:56,934
Those kinds of things will come up often in comprehensive permit hearings.

364
00:37:57,594 --> 00:38:00,135
They're outside the board's jurisdiction.

365
00:38:00,415 --> 00:38:10,239
And it's actually fairly easy to just sort of blame the subsidizing agency if you need to, because you really can't regulate anything that falls into these areas.

366
00:38:10,299 --> 00:38:14,901
The statute is really quite clear about what the ZBA's jurisdiction is.

367
00:38:15,042 --> 00:38:18,163
And I think that probably the most well-known

368
00:38:19,060 --> 00:38:34,190
case of a community where an approval of a comp permit went to court because the community was over 10% and some people were unhappy with the board's decision in that case, which was Boothroyd, the court was pretty clear.

369
00:38:34,270 --> 00:38:36,112
The jurisdiction of the statute doesn't change.

370
00:38:36,672 --> 00:38:41,175
It's simply the tilting of where does the burden fall?

371
00:38:41,675 --> 00:38:45,658
The balancing local concerns with regional need.

372
00:38:46,038 --> 00:38:48,440
But the actual scope of jurisdiction does not change.

373
00:38:49,890 --> 00:38:52,671
So the applicant has to give you certain things.

374
00:38:53,131 --> 00:39:00,413
They have to show that whether they're a public agency, a nonprofit, or a limited dividend organization, of course, the PEL kind of addresses these things.

375
00:39:01,114 --> 00:39:05,535
Evidence of site control, that all gets sort of embodied in the project eligibility letter.

376
00:39:06,215 --> 00:39:08,816
Of course, you should still look at the documents yourselves.

377
00:39:08,896 --> 00:39:14,518
I would never say just rely on the subsidizing agency, but the developer still has to give you that information.

378
00:39:15,410 --> 00:39:17,972
They have to provide what's called a preliminary plan.

379
00:39:19,093 --> 00:39:21,475
So it's really, it is, it's a preliminary plan.

380
00:39:21,515 --> 00:39:24,517
It's sort of how are we going to, what's going to be on this property?

381
00:39:24,557 --> 00:39:25,358
How many buildings?

382
00:39:25,818 --> 00:39:26,538
How many units?

383
00:39:27,079 --> 00:39:28,500
Where's the parking going to be?

384
00:39:28,620 --> 00:39:30,001
How are we dealing with stormwater?

385
00:39:32,103 --> 00:39:36,306
If the project does involve a subdivision, they have to submit a preliminary subdivision plan.

386
00:39:36,326 --> 00:39:41,030
They have to kind of show how utilities are going to be brought into the site and delivered to the units.

387
00:39:41,842 --> 00:39:50,428
And then very important, what waivers are they asking you to grant under the city's ordinances so that they can build their project?

388
00:39:51,529 --> 00:40:06,360
I will say that that waiver list will change over the course of the permitting process because as you work with the developer to try to get the best project you can for Medford, there may be some waivers that go away and some new ones that come in.

389
00:40:06,480 --> 00:40:10,443
So I don't encourage people to be too rigid about the

390
00:40:11,524 --> 00:40:27,312
details of the waiver list up front, but you certainly don't want to close the hearing without a very specific waiver list from the applicant that you can then act on and not be asked to just sort of approve waivers that would be necessary to build what's on the plans.

391
00:40:27,372 --> 00:40:30,153
I mean, you're entitled to more detail than that.

392
00:40:30,862 --> 00:40:32,384
We can talk about that more later.

393
00:40:32,404 --> 00:40:34,986
This is right out of the handbook, of course.

394
00:40:35,046 --> 00:40:55,563
I always just, I have to bring this up because I found myself a couple of times brought into communities where no one had dealt with 40B and the poor administrative assistant in the ZBA's office who was used to the public hearing timelines for special permits or variances had no idea that that hearing had to open in 30 days.

395
00:40:57,362 --> 00:40:59,564
and ended up with a constructive approval problem.

396
00:40:59,604 --> 00:41:10,050
So there are some specific dates that really are key, some of which you're going to be familiar with because of the situation that's kind of gone on with the general and area minimum assertion.

397
00:41:10,070 --> 00:41:12,732
You have to open the hearing within 30 days.

398
00:41:12,832 --> 00:41:14,053
That is in the statute.

399
00:41:14,153 --> 00:41:15,333
It's not regulatory.

400
00:41:15,434 --> 00:41:16,334
It can't be waived.

401
00:41:16,394 --> 00:41:20,817
The statute says if you don't do this, you will have a constructive approval problem on your hands.

402
00:41:21,703 --> 00:41:28,824
There's a timeline for notifying the developers that you are asserting you need a safe harbor, which I know you've been through this already.

403
00:41:30,045 --> 00:41:36,686
Whenever the hearing does start up again, if it does, you have 180 days to bring the hearing to a close.

404
00:41:36,906 --> 00:41:39,366
That is in the regulations, it's not in the statute.

405
00:41:40,186 --> 00:41:47,948
But it's pretty clearly adhered to and everybody kind of understands it from municipal councils to developers.

406
00:41:47,988 --> 00:41:48,348
It's 180 day period.

407
00:41:50,261 --> 00:41:50,966
for the hearing.

408
00:41:51,187 --> 00:41:52,878
Now, if you're getting close to the end,

409
00:41:53,720 --> 00:41:57,122
And it looks like you're going to be able to work some things out with a developer.

410
00:41:57,202 --> 00:41:59,343
They'll typically grant you a modest extension.

411
00:41:59,623 --> 00:42:01,123
They want to get a yes from you.

412
00:42:01,243 --> 00:42:04,865
So it's not really in their best interest to just become too rigid.

413
00:42:04,905 --> 00:42:08,167
But on the other hand, no one wants to drive a permitting process out for two years.

414
00:42:09,027 --> 00:42:19,212
Once you close that hearing, the statute then comes into play and says, you've got 40 days now to deliberate, reach a decision, file it with the city clerk.

415
00:42:19,832 --> 00:42:21,253
You've got 40 days to do that.

416
00:42:22,193 --> 00:42:28,837
there is a 20-day appeal period of your decision as there would be for any other kind of zoning action that you might be doing.

417
00:42:29,898 --> 00:42:41,804
So the 30-day open the hearing, the 180-day hearing requirement, and the 40 days to get the decision, you know, agreed to and filed with the clerk, those are all just really critical deadlines.

418
00:42:44,526 --> 00:42:51,530
Public hearing process, you know, I don't know who on the board is chair because I don't know any of you, but I will just say

419
00:42:52,924 --> 00:43:15,990
One of the best things I think you can do to help the public, to help everybody, is for the chair to spend a little time at the beginning of the hearing just explaining what this law is and why an application is in front of you and what your job is, just so people understand that there are things you have control over and things that the statute kind of displaces from local officials.

420
00:43:17,736 --> 00:43:26,200
It's important for people to understand that you really can't impose things on a 40B permit that you don't impose on anything else in the city.

421
00:43:26,380 --> 00:43:41,367
And I can just assure you that applicants, attorneys who know what they're doing, are very conscious of kind of what the city's procedures and policies are around waivers and so forth that may be customarily granted for some other kind of project.

422
00:43:42,793 --> 00:43:51,603
You know, if that's the case they'll push for it for a 40 B as well so you have to sort of be thinking about how would I handle this if this were not a 40 B, what would I be doing.

423
00:43:52,784 --> 00:43:57,149
They can't be treated differently from from other applications that come into the city.

424
00:43:58,140 --> 00:44:00,921
The municipal departments are critical, I find.

425
00:44:01,322 --> 00:44:02,782
They have a technical review role.

426
00:44:02,842 --> 00:44:21,091
They can be extremely helpful to the Board of Appeals, just in terms of reviewing the plans and helping you identify what's missing, what's good, what's inconsistent with something that's going on in the city that you need to know, get their involvement early on.

427
00:44:21,231 --> 00:44:27,434
I know back when, before the general land area minimum assertion had kicked in,

428
00:44:28,110 --> 00:44:45,497
I did meet with the city staff and I said to them, and I would say it again, their role in this process as helpful to the ZBA is just so important because they do technical review all the time and they can be very helpful to the board to sort of separate out what's a reasonable waiver request.

429
00:44:46,077 --> 00:44:48,999
What can you waive without really creating a harm?

430
00:44:49,579 --> 00:44:56,582
And what is something that really just should not be waived and that there's a valid health and safety reason to not waive something.

431
00:44:57,788 --> 00:45:01,729
helping you understand the consequences of perhaps, you know, the application.

432
00:45:04,390 --> 00:45:09,552
Again, this is just kind of reiterating that as well, that there's a lot of other things that staff can do too.

433
00:45:10,592 --> 00:45:22,396
Sometimes the public is very animated about Chapter 40B applications, so the extent to which you could just keep all the documents on the web where people can find them, then you're not going to be inundated with requests from public records.

434
00:45:24,297 --> 00:45:26,938
Helping the board understand what's a reasonable waiver,

435
00:45:27,766 --> 00:45:45,067
And also, and I would emphasize this as well, no matter how talented and capable your city staff are, and I think in Bedford you've got really extraordinary staff, there's a lot of merit in getting peer review consultants involved in reviewing these projects.

436
00:45:46,180 --> 00:46:08,890
If a project's going to go to the Housing Appeals Committee because there's a dispute, I would certainly rather see the board represented by independent consultants than folks who work for the city who are likely to be painted by the opposing developer as people with a vested interest or perhaps representing things from a political rather than a technical point of view.

437
00:46:10,510 --> 00:46:14,093
Peer review is just really, really helpful to ZBAs on these cases.

438
00:46:14,653 --> 00:46:24,419
You just get independent assistance and generally the developers would prefer it because it does reduce some of the politics in these cases.

439
00:46:26,340 --> 00:46:28,281
I'm just being honest with you, they tend to get political.

440
00:46:29,662 --> 00:46:32,424
You can retain peer review consultants at the applicant's expense.

441
00:46:33,413 --> 00:46:40,618
Their job is to review what the applicant has submitted, not to prepare new studies that the board may want.

442
00:46:42,559 --> 00:46:47,642
Everything they produce becomes part of the record, which can be very helpful to the board.

443
00:46:48,383 --> 00:47:00,551
The typical peer review services that I see are traffic, site simil, and often for multifamily, I would say especially for multifamily,

444
00:47:01,544 --> 00:47:03,845
architectural design review as well.

445
00:47:05,185 --> 00:47:06,546
So those are kind of fields.

446
00:47:06,686 --> 00:47:17,089
And to the earlier question about a pro forma review, I do cover that a little bit later in this presentation, but the board does have a right to kind of ask for changes in the project.

447
00:47:17,789 --> 00:47:22,110
And the applicant has a right to come back and say, that's going to make my project uneconomic.

448
00:47:22,190 --> 00:47:24,051
And you have a right to say, prove it to me.

449
00:47:25,191 --> 00:47:27,832
It's probably a good idea not to wait till day 170 to bring that up.

450
00:47:31,402 --> 00:47:37,665
But I will also say, I think anything you can do to avoid it is probably the best thing you can do.

451
00:47:37,745 --> 00:47:40,127
And I'll cover that in a few slides.

452
00:47:42,188 --> 00:47:51,753
During the hearing process and certainly during deliberations, focusing on kind of what are real issues that are jurisdictional will help you a lot.

453
00:47:52,433 --> 00:48:00,798
Instead of getting sidetracked on things that you really can't regulate anyway, you can request additional information from the applicant if you need it.

454
00:48:01,500 --> 00:48:12,251
Typically, they will provide it unless it's sort of extraordinary graphics that kind of help to establish the height or massing and setbacks, relationship to neighboring properties.

455
00:48:12,851 --> 00:48:23,782
Those are all fairly standard requests from ZBAs to applicants so that the board and the public understands how a proposed project is going to sit on the site and relate to its context.

456
00:48:27,838 --> 00:48:28,658
You can do this.

457
00:48:30,099 --> 00:48:40,401
The way it sort of works in the regulations is that you've done all the peer reviews that were possible and the developers had an opportunity to modify the proposal based on requests you've made.

458
00:48:40,421 --> 00:48:49,404
You kind of push back and say, well, we still want certain things that you haven't addressed.

459
00:48:50,044 --> 00:48:54,145
And the applicant says, I can't do that because you're going to make my project uneconomic.

460
00:48:54,967 --> 00:49:03,854
That's at the point where the board can say, let's take a look at a pro forma that would reflect what we're asking you to do.

461
00:49:05,155 --> 00:49:07,317
And we'll get someone to review it independently.

462
00:49:08,678 --> 00:49:12,561
That is certainly allowed under the 40B regulations.

463
00:49:12,801 --> 00:49:16,604
And I think sometimes it can be helpful.

464
00:49:17,585 --> 00:49:24,450
But I think anything you can do to keep the conversation focused on how to get the best project you can for your city,

465
00:49:25,484 --> 00:49:27,926
is probably the more productive thing to do.

466
00:49:28,707 --> 00:49:31,750
I'm certainly not averse to pro forma reviews.

467
00:49:31,850 --> 00:49:37,295
I'm just saying, honest to God, guys, I don't think I've ever seen it really yield anything helpful.

468
00:49:38,276 --> 00:49:49,607
I do think there's also the problem of the risk too, that you hire a qualified peer review consultant who comes back and says to you, the applicant's right.

469
00:49:50,284 --> 00:49:50,904
they can't do it.

470
00:49:51,364 --> 00:49:52,625
Now you've just lost your leverage.

471
00:49:52,665 --> 00:49:54,926
And I don't know that that really helps anybody.

472
00:49:55,046 --> 00:50:08,672
So I certainly understand and respect that you may reach a point where you need to do this, but I would say push as much as you can not to have to do this and just try to get the best that you can for your city.

473
00:50:08,792 --> 00:50:13,934
And if you need to do this, you do it, but if you can avoid it, it's probably best.

474
00:50:15,495 --> 00:50:22,357
The statute is very clear about the local concerns that you have the ability to sort of impose on the project.

475
00:50:23,437 --> 00:50:42,403
And those involve public health and public safety, environmental impact, design, site and building design both, open space in the context of the statute that really is around open space on the site, the adequacy of open space to serve the residents of the development.

476
00:50:43,627 --> 00:50:55,775
Um, planning concerns, but typically planning means, uh, you have a city master plan and you identify in that plan areas that are appropriate for affordable housing.

477
00:50:56,336 --> 00:50:58,157
And you've actually acted on that plan.

478
00:50:58,317 --> 00:51:05,762
So as a result of that master plan and the identification of areas that are appropriate, the city has actually caused housing to be built.

479
00:51:06,843 --> 00:51:13,007
That's, that's a planning realm that the housing appeals committee is clearly established is worth that they would pay attention to.

480
00:51:13,765 --> 00:51:25,750
but simply saying, well, we have an open space plan and we think we might like to buy that property or a master plan says this area is appropriate for commercial development and not for residential.

481
00:51:26,190 --> 00:51:31,393
The housing appeals committee will typically say, so where is the planning for housing and how have you addressed it?

482
00:51:31,473 --> 00:51:35,334
And if you haven't, then the planning argument pretty much gets set aside.

483
00:51:35,755 --> 00:51:38,696
I'm being pretty blunt with you, but that's just the way it works.

484
00:51:40,323 --> 00:51:47,850
There are, you know, the sort of general statutory recognition of quote other local concerns, but they're all really around the physical development of the site.

485
00:51:49,311 --> 00:52:01,682
When push comes to shove, the local concerns that are probably most paramount in the certainly everybody's mind, I think, in terms of the ability to deny one of these projects involves public health and public safety.

486
00:52:02,923 --> 00:52:06,066
The environmental considerations in a 40 B context.

487
00:52:06,877 --> 00:52:11,020
really are if the developer can comply with state environmental requirements.

488
00:52:12,402 --> 00:52:14,684
That's really all the statute requires them to do.

489
00:52:15,885 --> 00:52:18,507
It can be tricky sometimes for local officials.

490
00:52:19,027 --> 00:52:30,817
If there's a particular environmental concern where perhaps the state permit, whatever it may be, doesn't really adequately account for some local problem, then that becomes a separate matter.

491
00:52:31,525 --> 00:52:35,227
that's much more likely to happen in very small towns than in a city.

492
00:52:35,887 --> 00:52:43,791
But just to be clear, it's really around whether the project can satisfy the state environmental requirements that would apply to it.

493
00:52:44,832 --> 00:52:52,735
So public health and public safety, and to some extent, building and site design are probably the most important things for the board to try and get right.

494
00:52:54,616 --> 00:52:55,937
You have a couple of options.

495
00:52:55,997 --> 00:52:57,638
You can deny the application.

496
00:52:58,443 --> 00:53:01,145
You can approve it with conditions or approve it as is.

497
00:53:01,385 --> 00:53:12,273
I don't think I've ever seen an approval as is, except for a one unit 40B in a town on the Cape that was a Habitat for Humanity project.

498
00:53:12,353 --> 00:53:18,437
But there's a big difference between denial and approval with conditions.

499
00:53:18,597 --> 00:53:24,921
When a denial comes to the Housing Appeals Committee, the question really is, have you met your regional fair share?

500
00:53:26,442 --> 00:53:27,063
If you haven't,

501
00:53:27,769 --> 00:53:38,516
then you have a very strong burden imposed on you to show that the denial is based on factors that are more important than the regional housing need.

502
00:53:39,416 --> 00:53:50,023
That isn't quite the same as an approval with conditions where a developer's appealing to the Housing Appeals Committee because he or she is aggrieved by some condition you've put on the project.

503
00:53:50,943 --> 00:53:53,265
I'm not saying that that's a minor matter.

504
00:53:53,365 --> 00:53:55,006
I'm just saying denial is a much harder

505
00:53:57,586 --> 00:54:07,648
action to sustain, which is why you'll often hear attorneys advise DBAs, if you can figure out a way to approve it with conditions, it's probably the better thing to do.

506
00:54:08,228 --> 00:54:09,568
I try to sort of stay out of that.

507
00:54:09,608 --> 00:54:14,629
I think it's a policy call for the ZBA, but I just want you to be aware there is a difference.

508
00:54:16,330 --> 00:54:24,851
So if you don't have any safe harbor status, then the applicant who's unhappy can go to the Housing Appeals Committee.

509
00:54:25,992 --> 00:54:26,652
Other people

510
00:54:27,726 --> 00:54:30,527
who want to appeal would go to Superior Court or Land Court.

511
00:54:31,868 --> 00:54:37,311
If there is a safe harbor, then the developer can't appeal to the HAC, then all the appeals go to court.

512
00:54:37,971 --> 00:54:40,872
And that's sort of a big difference in the way the statute works.

513
00:54:40,892 --> 00:54:45,274
It was this assumption that somehow there would be this expedited administrative review.

514
00:54:45,294 --> 00:54:50,357
I think anybody who's dealt with the Housing Appeals Committee knows it's not an expedited review.

515
00:54:52,058 --> 00:54:54,959
So this is just like a summary of who's supposed to do what.

516
00:54:55,099 --> 00:54:56,540
Subsidizing agency has a role

517
00:54:57,291 --> 00:55:10,135
The select board or mayor are really the ones to sort of get the notice from the subsidizing agency and have the primary responsibility to provide the municipalities formal comments on the PEL application.

518
00:55:10,155 --> 00:55:21,799
The city departments should do their tactical review that they do so well and help the ZBA evaluate waivers that have been requested in order to build the project.

519
00:55:23,478 --> 00:55:29,962
And your job is to conduct a technical fair review, which I'm sure you do on any other matter that comes before you.

520
00:55:31,362 --> 00:55:36,665
Getting feedback and advice from peer review consultants, or if you decide not to do that, at least from city staff.

521
00:55:37,890 --> 00:55:43,452
running a public hearing process, which frankly can be sometimes quite difficult with these cases, and sometimes it's fine.

522
00:55:44,612 --> 00:56:03,058
And then going through that balancing test at the end, when you've gathered all the evidence that you would need to make a decision up or down, it's going through that process of vetting the local concerns against the regional need, and then preparing and voting on a decision within the timeframe established by the statute.

523
00:56:05,458 --> 00:56:08,659
So this little next section is about kind of what happens after all that.

524
00:56:08,679 --> 00:56:11,440
Do you want to take a moment to ask any questions right now?

525
00:56:15,702 --> 00:56:17,042
I see a hand up, Mike.

526
00:56:17,982 --> 00:56:18,823
Yeah.

527
00:56:20,043 --> 00:56:22,524
So Judy, on a prior slide, I think it might have been 25.

528
00:56:22,784 --> 00:56:24,845
Yeah.

529
00:56:24,985 --> 00:56:31,587
There was a bullet about, I think it was called working sessions that you didn't really speak to.

530
00:56:31,627 --> 00:56:33,728
So tell us a little bit about what those are.

531
00:56:34,525 --> 00:56:36,285
I can't believe I skipped over that, I'm sorry.

532
00:56:37,266 --> 00:56:44,968
So this is one of these things where you have to get input from your city solicitor or a special counsel.

533
00:56:46,008 --> 00:56:51,050
Because my experience is town attorneys do not agree on this, but the concept is this.

534
00:56:51,890 --> 00:57:03,673
Sometimes there are just purely technical matters that would be best to let the peer review consultants and the developer and the developer's consultants and city staff go into a room and try to

535
00:57:04,065 --> 00:57:09,910
at least understand what the options are for perhaps addressing a particular concern.

536
00:57:10,811 --> 00:57:15,575
And in some communities, a ZBA member may sit in on that working session.

537
00:57:15,695 --> 00:57:16,756
We call them working sessions.

538
00:57:17,376 --> 00:57:20,279
In some communities, the ZBA has nothing to do with it.

539
00:57:20,539 --> 00:57:26,444
And it kind of comes to the staff or the peer review consultants to come back to the board and say, this is what we discussed.

540
00:57:26,865 --> 00:57:28,706
This is what we recommend based on what we heard.

541
00:57:31,422 --> 00:57:34,403
There are some people who don't like the working session model.

542
00:57:34,423 --> 00:57:37,323
The concern is that it becomes kind of a shadow public hearing.

543
00:57:37,343 --> 00:57:48,046
I think the counter to that is that no decisions can be made because the only authority with the decision, the only authority to make a decision on a comprehensive permit is the ZBA.

544
00:57:49,206 --> 00:57:53,767
But I would say I've been involved in a lot of these often at the board's request.

545
00:57:53,947 --> 00:57:56,368
So the board did not have to get involved in the working session.

546
00:57:57,558 --> 00:58:08,061
I think some boards feel that's safer, that then they're not actually ever in a position where it looks like they're carrying out a conversation behind closed doors about a matter that is the subject of a public hearing.

547
00:58:08,782 --> 00:58:15,904
But if your council says, it's okay for one board member to sit in and you want to do that, then I will leave it to your council.

548
00:58:16,764 --> 00:58:19,005
I apologize if I skipped over that, I didn't mean to.

549
00:58:19,025 --> 00:58:21,686
Okay, thanks.

550
00:58:22,706 --> 00:58:23,186
No problem.

551
00:58:25,167 --> 00:58:25,747
Anybody else?

552
00:58:28,164 --> 00:58:29,205
Why don't I just keep going then?

553
00:58:31,867 --> 00:58:34,309
So the comprehensive permit is over.

554
00:58:35,210 --> 00:58:36,571
Everybody breathes a sigh of relief.

555
00:58:38,393 --> 00:58:43,077
Eventually, it goes back to the subsidizing agency for a process that's called final approval.

556
00:58:44,778 --> 00:58:53,826
And that is when the subsidizing agency issues the regulatory agreement and indeed riders for an ownership project.

557
00:58:54,790 --> 00:58:55,931
and a monitoring agreement.

558
00:58:55,971 --> 00:59:05,035
This is sort of when the legal agreements around the project, being a comprehensive permit development, are worked out with the developer and signed.

559
00:59:05,156 --> 00:59:09,778
And some of these, of course, will come to the ZBA to sign as well, and it's up to you whether you want to sign them.

560
00:59:09,878 --> 00:59:22,765
But between the subsidizing agency and the developer, these agreements are reached that govern the compliance responsibilities of the project in terms of affordable housing benefit,

561
00:59:23,540 --> 00:59:24,780
monitoring and so forth.

562
00:59:26,021 --> 00:59:33,504
During the final approval kind of phase, the developer will prepare a fair housing marketing plan.

563
00:59:34,564 --> 00:59:39,426
And the subsidizing agency will review and approve that or ask for changes for it.

564
00:59:39,466 --> 00:59:51,690
But that's when the kind of how are we going to solicit applications from people eligible for affordable housing and how are we going to pick tenants that all gets worked out in what's called an affirmative fair housing marketing plan.

565
00:59:52,485 --> 00:59:59,611
that gets into how do we pick eligible people and how are we going to make sure that it's a process that's fair.

566
01:00:00,792 --> 01:00:07,397
There is the limited dividend restriction that the for-profit developers will have to agree to, to limit profits.

567
01:00:08,077 --> 01:00:14,222
And then ultimately there is what comes much later is the final cost certification.

568
01:00:14,282 --> 01:00:16,084
I'll talk about that a little bit more in a minute.

569
01:00:16,124 --> 01:00:21,128
But all this sort of final approval packaging happens before the developer

570
01:00:21,803 --> 01:00:27,028
is going to come back to the city and say, I'm all set with the state, I want to build my project.

571
01:00:29,210 --> 01:00:42,042
A well-crafted comprehensive permit will have tiers of things that the developer has to do in order to even get to a building permit, like, you know, these are things you have to do before you can

572
01:00:43,282 --> 01:00:44,163
put a shovel in the ground.

573
01:00:44,303 --> 01:00:46,565
These are things you have to do before you can get a building permit.

574
01:00:46,905 --> 01:00:50,667
These are things you have to do before you're going to get any certificate of occupancy.

575
01:00:51,228 --> 01:00:53,649
These are things you have to do before you get your final CO.

576
01:00:54,110 --> 01:00:58,433
That all gets kind of laid out in the decision from you folks.

577
01:00:59,226 --> 01:01:03,548
Well, the developer ultimately has to come back and say, I've worked everything out with the state.

578
01:01:03,648 --> 01:01:04,888
Here's the regulatory agreement.

579
01:01:05,508 --> 01:01:07,229
It's going to be recorded at the registry.

580
01:01:07,669 --> 01:01:09,510
Here are my final plans for my project.

581
01:01:10,150 --> 01:01:19,073
And at that point, someone in city government, the building inspector, other city staff, often the peer review consultants are brought back for this.

582
01:01:19,673 --> 01:01:27,156
There's a review process for the so-called final plans to confirm that they are substantially consistent with the approved plans

583
01:01:27,656 --> 01:01:29,539
listed in the comprehensive permit decision.

584
01:01:31,582 --> 01:01:37,592
Once in a while, a developer may come back to you and say, I need a modification of my permit.

585
01:01:38,628 --> 01:01:47,655
Because when I finally got on the site and did more detailed investigations, I discovered some conditions that I didn't know existed and I'm going to have to change my plans.

586
01:01:48,496 --> 01:01:51,098
There's a process for an applicant to come back to you.

587
01:01:51,118 --> 01:01:54,901
But let's assume for the sake of this discussion, they don't need any amendments.

588
01:01:55,001 --> 01:02:01,687
So they're going to the building department with their plans, evidence of having worked everything out with the subsidizing agency.

589
01:02:02,267 --> 01:02:03,367
They build their project.

590
01:02:03,487 --> 01:02:10,288
There are inspections during construction that the municipality, of course, would be responsible for, as it would for any other type of development.

591
01:02:12,089 --> 01:02:30,212
If because of the scale or size or complexity of the project, the building department needs assistance, it is typically a provision in a comp permit that the developer will have to provide some financial support to hire specialized consultants to assist the building inspector if he or she wants that assistance.

592
01:02:31,761 --> 01:02:39,003
When they are finished with the project, there is this cost certification report that's filed with the subsidizing agency.

593
01:02:39,723 --> 01:02:40,783
You get a copy of it.

594
01:02:41,643 --> 01:02:47,324
It's the subsidizing agency's call whether the developer made too much money or not.

595
01:02:48,145 --> 01:02:52,306
But you are entitled to a copy of that so that you can review it.

596
01:02:53,206 --> 01:02:56,746
And if you have concerns about it, you can comment to the subsidizing agency.

597
01:02:57,887 --> 01:02:58,887
The developer has to

598
01:03:00,090 --> 01:03:02,512
you know, activate this permit within three years.

599
01:03:02,532 --> 01:03:06,615
They have to get going within three years or the permit will lapse.

600
01:03:08,137 --> 01:03:11,739
They are allowed to transfer the comprehensive permit to another entity.

601
01:03:12,200 --> 01:03:18,444
That typically is overseen by the subsidizing agency, but clearly the DBA and the municipality are notified.

602
01:03:19,585 --> 01:03:28,072
If there need to be changes to the permit because of things that develop or discovered after the fact, if it's a minor change,

603
01:03:28,674 --> 01:03:30,515
You can approve those administratively.

604
01:03:30,535 --> 01:03:32,236
There's no public hearing.

605
01:03:32,376 --> 01:03:34,278
You just respond in 20 days.

606
01:03:34,338 --> 01:03:35,759
They need a minor modification.

607
01:03:36,139 --> 01:03:37,420
It's consistent with the plans.

608
01:03:38,320 --> 01:03:40,302
There's no reason to reopen the public hearing.

609
01:03:40,402 --> 01:03:41,903
You just approve it administratively.

610
01:03:42,343 --> 01:03:47,627
If they want a substantial change, then you get to say, we're not going to approve this administratively.

611
01:03:48,247 --> 01:03:49,988
We're going to open a public hearing on this.

612
01:03:50,268 --> 01:03:54,291
And the public hearing focuses on the amendment that was a change.

613
01:03:54,311 --> 01:03:56,112
It doesn't reopen the whole project.

614
01:03:57,765 --> 01:03:59,507
And, you know, there are timelines for this.

615
01:03:59,767 --> 01:04:08,997
If you decide that it's not a minor modification, then you need to get right on it to open to public hearing again and the whole sort of 30 day thing, all of that kicks in again.

616
01:04:12,041 --> 01:04:14,282
And then the project, you know, ultimately is built.

617
01:04:14,322 --> 01:04:17,824
And as I said, the sort of final piece of this is the cost certification report.

618
01:04:19,125 --> 01:04:23,729
And it really is sort of the subsidizing agencies call to approve those so called.

619
01:04:24,609 --> 01:04:28,031
But you should get a copy and you should insist on a copy and you should review it.

620
01:04:28,952 --> 01:04:32,675
And certainly, your city staff should have a look at it as well.

621
01:04:33,135 --> 01:04:35,977
If you have comments on it to get into the subsidizing agency.

622
01:04:38,314 --> 01:04:49,841
The last piece of this is just around some fair housing issues, which I don't know that these matter especially to Medford, but I just wanna make sure you're aware of them.

623
01:04:51,182 --> 01:05:03,529
To count on the subsidized housing inventory, affordable housing has to be made available to income eligible people in sort of a large market area, typically the area that is used to set the income limits.

624
01:05:04,269 --> 01:05:07,311
So that's sort of a helpful or maybe not helpful way to think about

625
01:05:08,031 --> 01:05:12,593
You know, you've got this sort of greater Boston area within which you have a pretty integrated labor market.

626
01:05:13,313 --> 01:05:19,516
And so people who work in the Boston area might very much want to live in Medford for, you know, all the right reasons.

627
01:05:20,357 --> 01:05:26,339
So it's important to make sure that the outreach about the units reaches, you know, people beyond the city.

628
01:05:26,819 --> 01:05:27,900
At the same time, of course,

629
01:05:28,568 --> 01:05:32,809
you do have the ability to request a local preference consideration.

630
01:05:33,549 --> 01:05:41,691
My experience is, for the most part, if it's a well-reasoned request, the state has generally approved them.

631
01:05:41,851 --> 01:05:45,752
There have been a few times when I think they've probably approved them, but not for 70%.

632
01:05:47,813 --> 01:05:51,734
But it really is the municipality's job to defend the request, not the developer's.

633
01:05:52,354 --> 01:05:54,814
So some local preference will be allowed.

634
01:05:55,115 --> 01:05:56,575
And local preference means

635
01:05:57,910 --> 01:06:09,674
that a person lives in Medford now, or works for the city, or works for some other employer in Medford, or doesn't live in Medford but has children in the Medford public schools.

636
01:06:10,175 --> 01:06:16,317
Those are basically the defining criteria for local preference.

637
01:06:18,337 --> 01:06:30,466
So back to this whole idea about kind of regional housing needs and regional disparities, the passage of this law in connection with the Federal Fair Housing Act was not a coincidence.

638
01:06:30,506 --> 01:06:35,870
There was so much going on in the 1960s that kind of brought all these things into the fore.

639
01:06:37,271 --> 01:06:46,337
Chapter 40B, whether we like it or not, sort of functions as a check on local requirements that make affordable housing difficult to build in some communities.

640
01:06:47,198 --> 01:06:53,780
And so over time, the Commonwealth has implemented some policies to try to further those purposes.

641
01:06:53,980 --> 01:07:00,121
And I would say, folks, a lot of this has been fairly recent in the last 10 to 15 years.

642
01:07:01,361 --> 01:07:07,903
So one of the things the Commonwealth has to do every few years is an analysis of impediments to fair housing choice.

643
01:07:09,723 --> 01:07:12,824
And in 2013, I think there was a real sort of,

644
01:07:13,580 --> 01:07:23,436
Recognition in the analysis of impediments process that 40 be itself unwittingly was contributing to some of these inequities that exist.

645
01:07:25,099 --> 01:07:26,481
So that, for example.

646
01:07:28,111 --> 01:07:33,495
it had become very difficult to build multifamily housing just about anywhere without 4EB.

647
01:07:34,096 --> 01:07:37,899
And so what is the Commonwealth's responsibility for that?

648
01:07:38,819 --> 01:07:44,464
Another aspect of this analysis of impediments was, guess what?

649
01:07:45,084 --> 01:07:49,087
Even where multifamily housing is being built, a lot of it is age-restricted.

650
01:07:49,207 --> 01:07:55,412
And so the people who most need help, or a group that does need help, which is younger families,

651
01:07:57,094 --> 01:07:59,276
in the workforce can't find housing.

652
01:08:00,236 --> 01:08:04,439
And the only way they're getting it is if it's permitted under the comprehensive permit law.

653
01:08:05,079 --> 01:08:07,041
And so does that make sense?

654
01:08:07,221 --> 01:08:12,424
What are we allowing communities to do through their zoning that might create a fair housing problem?

655
01:08:14,166 --> 01:08:20,690
And then kind of out of that came some policies from DHCD and also from the Attorney General's office.

656
01:08:21,430 --> 01:08:24,512
The DHCD policies directly affect you

657
01:08:25,568 --> 01:08:29,412
what the Attorney General does not, for reasons that I'll explain momentarily.

658
01:08:30,753 --> 01:08:43,547
DHCD adopted a policy coming out of this analysis of impediments process to address the imbalance of age-restricted housing, which really is a history to this that I don't want to bore you with.

659
01:08:43,627 --> 01:08:44,408
But in 1995, Congress

660
01:08:46,282 --> 01:08:55,849
amended the Fair Housing Act under the Housing for Older Persons Act that made it a lot easier for developers to build age-restricted housing for so-called active adults.

661
01:08:56,690 --> 01:09:06,837
So it opened up the door for a lot of development that was intended for households 55 and over, put in parentheses, without children.

662
01:09:07,958 --> 01:09:10,359
And so DHCD said, well, we've got to do something about this.

663
01:09:11,513 --> 01:09:40,537
So now what the state is doing and has been for the last couple of years is saying, okay, if you have a local bylaw or ordinance that supports the development of age restricted housing and your age restriction is so onerous that it bars people under 18 from living in the development, we will not approve your affordable units that you create under your zoning because your zoning is discriminatory against children.

664
01:09:41,685 --> 01:09:55,353
So, this may not have ever come up with you folks but has come up in a number of communities that I work in, where the age restricted housing really went beyond what what the literal language of the Housing for Older Persons Act

665
01:09:56,615 --> 01:10:04,840
and said not only does at least one person in the household have to be 55 and over, but everybody does, and nobody under 18 can live here.

666
01:10:05,380 --> 01:10:06,721
And that's just going too far.

667
01:10:06,841 --> 01:10:09,282
So DHCD has said, we're not going to approve these units.

668
01:10:09,362 --> 01:10:13,845
If that's what your zoning requires, don't ask us to put them on the subsidized housing inventory.

669
01:10:15,130 --> 01:10:29,422
The other thing that came out of it was this recognition again of the absence of family units that DHCD said we're not going to approve any more comprehensive permits where they don't have at least 10% of the units as three bedroom units.

670
01:10:29,462 --> 01:10:36,148
And I know this came up in an earlier question from Medford, like, well, what do we do if our need is mainly in smaller units?

671
01:10:37,423 --> 01:10:50,328
I've certainly seen in communities very close to you some developments where there were a lot of one-bedroom units and some twos and the obligatory 10% three, but the emphasis was on the smaller units.

672
01:10:50,448 --> 01:10:59,172
So it is possible certainly for developers to produce the smaller units, but as for including 10% three bedroom, they have no choice.

673
01:10:59,472 --> 01:11:01,052
That is a DHCD policy.

674
01:11:01,433 --> 01:11:02,613
They simply do not waive it.

675
01:11:05,094 --> 01:11:09,397
And the last piece of this, this is where it doesn't apply to you, but I want you to be aware of it.

676
01:11:11,079 --> 01:11:19,185
The analysis of impediments process said, well, what else can state government do beyond trying to sort of set policy for 40 Bs?

677
01:11:20,045 --> 01:11:29,012
And what happens in Massachusetts when towns adopt zoning, it doesn't go into effect until the attorney general's office blesses it.

678
01:11:29,172 --> 01:11:31,174
So there's a review process that happens.

679
01:11:31,870 --> 01:11:46,021
where zoning that's approved at a town meeting has to go to the attorney general's office, the municipal law unit for review for consistency with the statute and consistency with prevailing case law.

680
01:11:46,622 --> 01:11:49,504
Cities don't have to go through that attorney general review.

681
01:11:49,524 --> 01:11:51,526
That's why I'm saying this doesn't directly affect you.

682
01:11:51,986 --> 01:11:57,391
But I want you to be aware of it because people are watching for zoning that goes too far.

683
01:11:58,453 --> 01:12:08,153
So out of the Attorney General's office in the last few years, there have been these fair housing cautions from the Attorney General's office to town saying.

684
01:12:08,930 --> 01:12:12,332
You've gone too far under the Federal Fair Housing Act, you may have a problem.

685
01:12:12,752 --> 01:12:15,113
And most of these, I mean I know these.

686
01:12:15,854 --> 01:12:16,474
It's kind of funny.

687
01:12:16,974 --> 01:12:27,139
Most of the letters of comment from the Attorney General's office have been around to bedroom restrictions, you know so nothing over two bedrooms or age restrictions that bar people under 18.

688
01:12:28,761 --> 01:12:33,983
floor area limits in units that would have the effect of disqualifying families from living there.

689
01:12:35,164 --> 01:12:38,145
This has really sort of become very much a focus from the Commonwealth.

690
01:12:38,905 --> 01:12:42,447
So that focus is why that 10% rule exists.

691
01:12:43,187 --> 01:12:51,370
And again, it may seem a little bit inconsistent with Medford's sense of its housing needs, and I'm not debating what your needs are.

692
01:12:51,390 --> 01:12:55,152
I'm just saying, when the developer says, I have to do this, they're not lying to you.

693
01:12:56,906 --> 01:13:02,370
So those are my slides and I'm happy to answer any questions at this point.

694
01:13:02,930 --> 01:13:07,154
I can leave these up or I can take them down so we can all see each other, whatever you prefer.

695
01:13:09,415 --> 01:13:11,477
And I see a hand up and it's Jamie.

696
01:13:13,378 --> 01:13:13,818
Thanks, Judy.

697
01:13:13,838 --> 01:13:19,863
So going on the three bedroom 10% requirement, I know this has come up with the MBTA communities requirement.

698
01:13:20,935 --> 01:13:40,124
With the 20% and 25% affordable and that 10% three bedrooms, is there guidance on whether those three bedrooms are required to be affordable or 20% of those three bedrooms being affordable or does the developer have their control on which units are going to be affordable?

699
01:13:40,844 --> 01:13:44,546
The developer is not supposed to be able to

700
01:13:46,660 --> 01:13:49,602
skew the allocation of the affordable units.

701
01:13:49,702 --> 01:13:54,285
The policy is that they are evenly distributed across all unit types and sizes.

702
01:13:56,907 --> 01:14:05,792
And I actually did see a subsidizing agency push back once when a developer was trying to make something particular type of unit in a project all market.

703
01:14:06,472 --> 01:14:08,474
And the subsidizing agency said, you can't do that.

704
01:14:08,594 --> 01:14:11,916
So there's been a healthy pushback, which I think is good.

705
01:14:11,936 --> 01:14:13,877
Yeah, absolutely.

706
01:14:18,260 --> 01:14:19,000
Other questions?

707
01:14:21,041 --> 01:14:26,323
Judy, I'm just gonna take a quick look and see if there, I'm not seeing any other hands.

708
01:14:26,343 --> 01:14:31,124
Do you want me to stop the sharing by the way on this so we can all see each other or do you need me to keep the slides up?

709
01:14:31,564 --> 01:14:33,085
No, you can take it down, that's great, thanks.

710
01:14:34,845 --> 01:14:40,567
Folks, if anybody does have a question now is the time, just raise your hand and we can unmute you.

711
01:14:44,569 --> 01:14:45,829
I am not seeing any.

712
01:14:48,669 --> 01:14:49,830
Judy, that was really wonderful.

713
01:14:49,870 --> 01:14:50,610
Thank you so much.

714
01:14:51,531 --> 01:14:52,231
Oh, well, thank you.

715
01:14:52,251 --> 01:14:54,953
I think you'll probably be seeing me again.

716
01:14:54,973 --> 01:14:55,533
I don't know.

717
01:14:55,553 --> 01:14:56,774
I guess we'll find out.

718
01:14:56,794 --> 01:14:58,395
That seems very likely.

719
01:14:59,847 --> 01:15:00,248
Okay.

720
01:15:00,488 --> 01:15:01,288
Yeah.

721
01:15:02,049 --> 01:15:07,412
So, um, I think you mentioned for you weren't sure I'm the chair and what you were saying about opening the meetings.

722
01:15:07,472 --> 01:15:08,953
I think that's really helpful advice.

723
01:15:10,354 --> 01:15:15,358
And then apart from that, we're all new within the last two or so years.

724
01:15:15,978 --> 01:15:20,501
And I think actually apart from that and myself, everyone's new this year.

725
01:15:21,407 --> 01:15:30,052
So this is really helpful to get everybody, all of us, myself included, up to speed on some of this stuff, because we've got some of these coming in the pipeline.

726
01:15:30,772 --> 01:15:31,532
I know, I know.

727
01:15:31,552 --> 01:15:33,693
All right, well, let me know what you need.

728
01:15:33,713 --> 01:15:34,814
All right, we will.

729
01:15:34,834 --> 01:15:35,414
Thank you so much.

730
01:15:35,454 --> 01:15:36,215
Really appreciate it.

731
01:15:36,955 --> 01:15:37,215
Okay.

732
01:15:37,235 --> 01:15:37,776
All right.

733
01:15:37,936 --> 01:15:38,616
Thank you so much.

734
01:15:38,996 --> 01:15:39,456
Have a good night.

735
01:15:40,097 --> 01:15:40,257
Yep.

736
01:15:40,397 --> 01:15:40,697
Take care.

737
01:15:40,717 --> 01:15:41,818
Bye bye.

738
01:15:42,258 --> 01:15:42,498
Bye bye.

739
01:15:50,130 --> 01:15:52,131
Just to let people know, my name is Dennis McDougall.

740
01:15:52,191 --> 01:15:53,131
I staff the zoning board.

741
01:15:53,171 --> 01:15:55,992
So I'm just going to throw my email address up on the screen.

742
01:15:56,012 --> 01:15:58,973
So if anybody's watching it on TV at home or anything, you can write that down.

743
01:15:58,993 --> 01:16:01,474
If you have any questions, you know, send me an email.

744
01:16:01,694 --> 01:16:03,915
I'd be happy to get you the answer as soon as I can.

745
01:16:05,055 --> 01:16:06,415
Just making sure I actually spelled it right.

746
01:16:08,456 --> 01:16:08,616
Yep.

747
01:16:08,656 --> 01:16:08,976
All right.

748
01:16:10,197 --> 01:16:13,898
Sometimes I forget the second L, especially when I'm typing quickly.

749
01:16:14,945 --> 01:16:16,791
Well then, no problem.

750
01:16:17,153 --> 01:16:18,638
Dennis, thanks so much.

751
01:16:18,778 --> 01:16:20,203
Okay, I think that's it, folks.

752
01:16:20,223 --> 01:16:20,525
Thank you.

